\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Rotating one side of a clock cut}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{On the orbit of #{N\ge2} unit spins in \ref{fdip-00ZX}, start with a
constant configuration. Choose a subset of vertices and rotate its
spins one position forward, leaving all other spins fixed. Let
#{E_{\rm cut}} consist of the labeled edges with exactly one endpoint
in this subset. The inner product stays #{1} off the cut and becomes
#{\cos(2\pi /N)} on each crossing, in either orientation. With site
weights one, the resulting hopping weight divided by the constant
weight is
##{\ue^{-(1-\cos(2\pi /N))\sum_{e\in E_{\rm cut}}w_e}.}
For the sign orbit (#{N=2}) the exponent is
#{-2\sum_{e\in E_{\rm cut}}w_e}.}
